Abstract

The Human Musical Vibration Atlas (HMVA) proposes a unified mathematical framework for modelling the physical, biological, cognitive, and cultural dimensions of music across human societies. In this revision we abandon the earlier fiber‑bundle metaphor in favour of a stratified structure whose base space is a manifold of statistical acoustic features and whose fibres are tradition‑specific local model spaces—discrete‑pitch orbifolds, continuous spectral manifolds, or rhythmic‑automaton complexes. Cross‑stratum comparisons are restricted to the base coordinates; within a single stratum, traditions can be compared through explicit morphisms of their internal geometries. We correct prior over‑claims: neural entrainment is described with a Hopf‑oscillator network whose Arnold tongues are analysed in both low‑ and high‑order regimes, and we explicitly separate dynamical from perceptual claims. Measurement bias is framed as a measurable residual rather than an eliminated quantity, and cross‑tradition distance is defined only within a stratum via structural metrics, not via an ungrounded latent embedding. All claims are theoretical; no experiments have been performed.

Keywords: music information geometry, stratified spaces, neural entrainment, cross‑cultural music analysis, computational ethnomusicology, measurement bias

