1. Introduction

Every human culture organizes sound into patterns that convey emotion, meaning, and identity. These patterns are physically measurable as acoustic vibrations, yet their significance is irreducibly cultural. The challenge of building a scientific atlas of the world’s musical traditions is therefore twofold: (1) to identify the shared physical and biological constraints within which all music operates, and (2) to represent the irreducible structural diversity of each tradition without imposing an external normative frame.

Previous large‑scale projects have taken sharply different approaches. Lomax’s Cantometrics [1] coded song performance styles globally but used pre‑defined Western‑derived categories. The CompMusic project [2] deliberately rejected cross‑cultural comparison, building separate computational models for Hindustani, Carnatic, Turkish makam, Arab‑Andalusian, and Beijing opera traditions on their own terms. Savage et al. [3] analysed 304 recordings from nine regions and found statistical universals—features that are probabilistically consistent across cultures, not absolute. Mehr et al. [4] demonstrated both universal patterns and profound cultural variation in song form and function. None of these efforts provides a common mathematical language capable of expressing how traditions differ while remaining comparable at the structural level.

The HMVA fills this gap by defining a formal object—a stratified space where each musical tradition occupies a point in a shared statistical base manifold and carries a local model space that captures its internal pitch, rhythm, and form geometry. Crucially, we recognize that the internal structures of different traditions can be of fundamentally different types (e.g., discrete pitch classes vs. continuous glissandi), so the “fibre” cannot be a single universal topological space. Instead, we stratify the base space into regions whose associated model spaces are of a common type, permitting structural comparison within a stratum while avoiding a false assumption of global uniformity. This structure enables comparison without reduction: one can ask whether two traditions share topological features (e.g., the presence of a cyclic rhythmic group) without judging them on a universal scale of complexity.

This document presents the revised mathematical skeleton of the HMVA (v0.3.0). Every layer of the original five‑layer architecture is populated with concrete, corrected models, and we explicitly address the problem of measurement bias in computational pipelines. No experimental validation has been performed; the paper remains a theoretical blueprint.

