2. Formal Architecture: A Stratified Model Space

We abandon the earlier claim that the HMVA is a fiber bundle with a global typical fibre. Instead, we model it as a base manifold B partitioned into disjoint strata, with each stratum equipped with a model space that captures the geometric structure of traditions of that type. The space of all traditions is not a bundle but a labelled collection of local models anchored to points in B; this is a stratified partition with local geometric data, sometimes called a stratified space of models.

Definition 2.1 (Musical Event).
A musical event is a tuple e = (t, \mathbf{f}, \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{c}) where t \in \mathbb{R} is time, \mathbf{f} is a time‑varying spectral representation, \mathbf{a} is a vector of articulatory features, and \mathbf{c} is a context label. For most of the atlas we work with a reduced acoustic vector \mathbf{s}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d, preserving the full tuple for cultural annotation.

Definition 2.2 (Musical Tradition).
A musical tradition \mathcal{T}_i is a generative model of culturally valid sequences of acoustic events, specified by a probabilistic grammar over a finite set of tradition‑specific abstract symbols (pitch classes, rhythmic durations) together with a surface mapping to continuous acoustic parameters. For concreteness, we adopt a hidden semi‑Markov model (HSMM) that emits event symbols with state‑dependent duration distributions and transitions. The choice of grammar class is fixed to HSMMs in this version to make the framework concrete; extensions to other formalisms remain possible. A toy example is given in Section 3.1.

Definition 2.3 (Statistical Universals Base Space).
Let a set of m acoustic features be chosen that exhibit statistically significant clustering across traditions when sampling is controlled (following Savage et al. [3] and Mehr et al. [4]). Each feature j is a discrete random variable taking values in a finite set of size k_j; its empirical distribution over all traditions defines a point in the probability simplex \Delta_{k_j}. The base space is the product B = \Delta_{k_1} \times \Delta_{k_2} \times \dots \times \Delta_{k_m}. To equip B with a Riemannian metric that respects the probabilistic nature of the coordinates, we use the Fisher information metric of the underlying multinomial distributions, following Amari & Nagaoka [5]. Because the features are not independent—Savage et al. report a network of inter‑correlations—we replace the naive product metric with the Fisher metric of a joint log‑linear model that includes pairwise interactions; this yields a metric tensor that incorporates the empirical covariance structure. The base space captures only low‑dimensional statistical tendencies—deliberately impoverished to avoid imposing a universal music‑theoretic vocabulary.

Each tradition \mathcal{T}_i is assigned a base point b_i \in B representing its empirical feature probabilities estimated from a representative corpus.

Definition 2.4 (Fiber Type Classification).
We recognise at least three distinct classes of internal musical geometry (the “fibre types”):

· Type D (Discrete pitch): The tradition organises pitch into a finite set of classes and uses a cyclic or hierarchical meter. The internal geometry is an orbifold quotient of the pitch‑time plane P = \mathbb{R}^2 by a discrete symmetry group (octave, transposition, rhythmic periodicity). Voice‑leading distances are geodesic distances in this quotient.
· Type C (Continuous pitch/spectral): The tradition employs continuous pitch glides, microtonal inflections, or timbre‑based organisation without fixed discrete pitches. The internal geometry is a manifold of spectral envelope shapes modulo time‑warping.
· Type R (Rhythmic/percussive dominant): Pitch is absent or secondary; the tradition is built from complex rhythmic timelines and timbral patterns. The internal geometry is a space of rhythmic automata or metric‑circle products without pitch orbifolds.

A musical tradition is labelled by its fibre type. Other types may be added as needed.

Definition 2.5 (Stratified Partition with Local Models).
The HMVA is a structure (E, \pi, B, \mathcal{S}) where:

· The base manifold B is as in Definition 2.3.
· \mathcal{S} is a partition of B into disjoint subsets B_{\text{D}}, B_{\text{C}}, B_{\text{R}}, \dots called strata, such that every tradition assigned to a stratum shares the same fibre type.
· For each stratum B_{\text{type}} there is a fixed model space M_{\text{type}} that serves as the typical internal geometry for traditions of that type (e.g., an orbifold for Type D, a spectral manifold for Type C).
· E is the collection of all traditions; a tradition \mathcal{T}_i is a base point b_i \in B together with a local model consisting of a concrete generative grammar and a structure map from the internal symbol sequences to M_{\text{type}} that respects the tradition‑specific symmetries.
· The projection \pi: E \to B maps a tradition to its base point.

There is no requirement that the pre‑image of a point in a stratum be homeomorphic to M_{\text{type}}; instead, all traditions in the same stratum can be embedded into M_{\text{type}} and compared through the geometry of that fixed model space. Cross‑stratum comparisons are possible only through the common base coordinates. This formulation captures the core hypothesis without over‑reaching into a full fibre‑bundle structure, for which gluing data across strata would be unavailable in the absence of natural stratum boundaries in cultural space.

