3. Layer‑Specific Mathematical Models

3.1 Layer I — Physical Vibrations and Geometric Pitch Spaces

The acoustic signal is a superposition of quasi‑sinusoidal components; time‑frequency analysis yields a time‑varying amplitude and frequency vector. For Type D traditions we adopt quotient spaces of the continuous pitch‑time plane.

Definition 3.1 (Pitch‑Time Plane).
Let pitch be represented as log‑frequency \phi = \log_2(f / f_0). The pitch‑time plane is P = \mathbb{R}^2 with coordinates (\phi, t).

Definition 3.2 (Tradition‑Specific Equivalence for Type D).
A Type‑D tradition defines a group G of symmetries acting on P. Common symmetries include octave equivalence (\phi \sim \phi + 1), transposition invariance (\phi \mapsto \phi + \alpha), and rhythmic periodicity (t \mapsto t + T). The geometric pitch space is the quotient P / G. For Western tonality, Tymoczko’s [6] OPTIC symmetries (octave, permutation, transposition, inversion, cardinality equivalence) yield an orbifold whose points are chord types. Voice‑leading distances are geodesic distances in the quotient.

Proposition 3.1 (Local Geometry for a Type‑D Tradition).
For a tradition with a finite set of pitch classes and a cyclic meter of period T, and assuming that pitch and rhythmic symmetries act independently, the quotient space is a product of a discrete orbifold and a circle, with a metric inherited from the Euclidean metric on P. Voice‑leading can be modelled by piecewise‑linear paths constrained to the chosen pitch classes.

If the symmetries are coupled—for instance, in traditions where rhythmic subdivision depends on the melodic mode (as in raga–tala interactions)—the quotient is not a product. The correct quotient is the total space of the joint equivalence relation: the set of orbits under the semidirect product of the two groups with the coupling specified. An explicit construction is possible by taking the product of the two groups and modding out by the isotropy subgroup induced by the coupling constraint. The resulting metric is the quotient metric inherited from P under this larger group action.

Toy Example — A Minimal Type‑D Tradition.
Consider a synthetic tradition with three pitch classes labelled \{\text{C}, \text{E}, \text{G}\} (a major triad), an octave periodicity, and a simple duple meter with period T = 2 beats. Pitch classes are identified after octave equivalence; the pitch group is generated by translation by 1 (octave) and transposition by any multiple of the basic interval (though here we restrict transpositions to those that permute the three tones). The rhythmic group is generated by t \mapsto t + 2. The joint quotient P/G is then the product of the discrete orbifold of the three‑note pitch class set (a three‑point space with the metric induced by the embedding in the circle \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}) and the circle \mathbb{R}/2\mathbb{Z}. The HSMM generative model consists of states for each pitch class, with self‑transition probabilities and duration distributions over multiples of the basic pulse. A musical sequence is a path in this orbifold; voice‑leading distance between two chord sequences is the minimal total Euclidean distance in the unfolded plane consistent with the grammar. While elementary, this example demonstrates how the abstract formalism anchors to a concrete model.

3.2 Layer II — Biological Entrainment as Coupled Oscillators

Mounting neuroscientific evidence suggests that auditory‑motor entrainment can be modelled as phase‑locking of nonlinear neural oscillators [7]. We formalise this using the Hopf normal form.

Definition 3.3 (Neural Oscillator Node).
The complex amplitude z(t) \in \mathbb{C} of a neural oscillator obeys

\dot{z} = z\left( \alpha + i\omega + \beta |z|^2 \right) + c \, e^{i\psi} \, s(t) + \eta(t),

where \alpha controls the bifurcation (oscillatory for \alpha > 0), \omega is the intrinsic frequency, \beta < 0 ensures saturation, s(t) is a filtered acoustic envelope, c is coupling strength, \psi a phase offset, and \eta(t) is neural noise.

Proposition 3.2 (Arnold Tongue Width and Perceptual Relevance).
For a periodic drive with frequency \Omega, the Hopf oscillator can exhibit stable p\!:\!q mode locking. The width of the locking region (the Arnold tongue) depends on the drive amplitude and the ratio. In the weak‑forcing limit, standard perturbation methods yield the classical scaling

\left| \frac{p}{q}\omega - \Omega \right| \leq K_{p,q} \, c^{q},

which shows that higher‑q resonances (complex integer ratios) have tongues that shrink rapidly with coupling strength. However, for high‑q ratios at fixed coupling, a separate asymptotic regime appears: the tongue width scales as q^{-3} (see, e.g., Pikovsky et al. [8], Ch. 7). This means that very high‑order ratios are dynamically fragile both because of rapid c^q suppression and an algebraic decay in q. The combination makes stable entrainment at ratios like 5:3 or 7:4 far less likely than at 2:1 or 3:2, providing a formal dynamical explanation for the cross‑cultural prevalence of simple integer ratios reported by Savage et al. [3].

Crucially, dynamical entrainment does not automatically equate to perceptual salience. The link from oscillator locking to conscious preference is a separate, testable hypothesis (see Large et al. [7] for a detailed discussion of the neural underpinnings of beat perception). We therefore present the Arnold‑tongue analysis as a constraint on the physical possibility of entrainment, not as a theory of aesthetic value.

Extension to Multiple Oscillators.
Consider a network of N oscillators with diffusive coupling:

\dot{z}_j = z_j(\alpha_j + i\omega_j + \beta_j |z_j|^2) + \sum_k A_{jk} (z_k - z_j) + c_j s(t).

Such networks are known to support a rich repertoire: synchronisation, amplitude death, and oscillation death [9]. Rather than claiming guaranteed stable synchrony—a claim that does not hold generically—we note that the network can exhibit both coordinated phase locking and complete suppression of oscillations. This makes the model suitable for capturing both cross‑cultural entrainment and documented instances of rhythmic non‑entrainment (e.g., in highly complex polyrhythms). A full master‑stability‑function analysis mapping the stable regimes is future work.

3.3 Layers III/IV — Information‑Theoretic Cognitive and Cultural Dynamics

Expectation and surprise are central to musical cognition. The IDyOM framework [10] models expectation as an information content (IC) derived from statistical learning. We recast this within each tradition.

Definition 3.4 (Tradition‑Conditional Information Content).
For a tradition \mathcal{T}_i with HSMM generating sequences of symbols \mathbf{e} = e_1, e_2, \dots, let P_i(e_t \mid e_{1:t-1}) be the predictive distribution. The information content of event e_t is

\text{IC}_i(t) = -\log_2 P_i(e_t \mid e_{1:t-1}).

This quantity is purely internal to the tradition and measures how predictable the next event is under the learned model. It is not a universal complexity measure; high IC can simply indicate a poorly trained model or a genuinely unpredictable stylistic feature, neither of which confers any value judgement. (We note that prior literature has sometimes misread IDyOM‑derived IC as a proxy for musical “complexity,” a practice we explicitly disavow.)

Cross‑Tradition Comparison Within a Stratum.
Because traditions in different strata have incommensurable internal symbol spaces, we do not attempt to define a universal distance between their information‑theoretic profiles. Instead, we restrict quantitative comparison to traditions within the same stratum, where a common model space M_{\text{type}} exists. For Type D traditions, each tradition’s HSMM and pitch‑time orbifold define a metric geometry. We can measure structural dissimilarity between two Type D traditions by comparing their orbifolds using the Gromov–Hausdorff distance (suitably adapted for labelled orbifolds) or by comparing their transition matrices and metric embeddings via optimal transport in the joint space of pitch and time. Such metrics respect the internal symmetries of each tradition and do not require a synthetic shared embedding.

For traditions of different types, only the base coordinates are available for comparison; the stratified architecture thus explicitly encodes the CompMusic lesson that a single feature space cannot capture all musical concepts.

3.4 Layer V — Computational Pipeline and Bias Correction

Any computational analysis begins with a choice of audio representation. The default mel‑spectrogram embeds a Western psychoacoustic bias [11]. Recent work by Chauhan & Pundhir [12] demonstrates a 15.7% F1‑score gap between Western and non‑Western music classification tasks when using mel‑scale features, and shows that culture‑adapted constant‑Q transforms (CQT) can cut this gap substantially. This systematic bias would corrupt the atlas if left unaddressed.

Definition 3.5 (Measurement Bias Operator — Relational Formulation).
Let \mathcal{A} be a standard feature extractor (e.g., a mel‑filterbank followed by a generic neural network). For a musical excerpt x from tradition i, let \hat{y} = \mathcal{A}(x) be its output. Define a culture‑informed encoder E_i trained with domain‑expert guidance, and consider its output y_i^{\text{cult}} = E_i(x) as a reference representation. The bias relative to this culture‑informed reference is

B_i(x) = \mathcal{A}(x) - y_i^{\text{cult}}(x),

which is measurable. The goal of bias correction is to reduce B_i(x) across a corpus, not to eliminate it entirely—perfect correction is impossible because E_i is itself an approximation.

Proposition 3.4 (Bias Mitigation via Culture‑Specific Front‑ends).
Replace the mel‑filterbank with a variable‑resolution constant‑Q transform (VQT) whose frequency bins are aligned with the tradition’s pitch interval system. For a tradition using, e.g., 24‑tone equal temperament, a filterbank with 24 bins per octave centred on the tradition’s reference pitch yields a representation that is approximately equivariant under the tradition’s transposition group. Chauhan & Pundhir [12] report that such front‑ends reduce the performance gap by roughly 52%, leaving a non‑zero residual bias \tilde{B}_i^{\text{res}}. We incorporate this residual into the bias operator and treat it as an irreducible uncertainty in any cross‑cultural comparison.

Pipeline Formalization.
For each tradition i, we define a culture‑aware encoder E_i: \text{Waveform} \to Z_i, where Z_i is a latent space adapted to the tradition’s pitch/rhythm classes. The HMVA computational layer comprises:

1. A menu of such encoders.
2. A bias‑correction block that estimates the systematic offset of a generic front‑end relative to E_i and applies an affine transformation to align their statistics. The residual bias is reported as a confidence measure.
3. For traditions within the same stratum, an alignment module maps the internal representations into the common model space M_{\text{type}} using known symmetries, enabling structural comparisons.

No adversarial latent embedding across strata is attempted; the stratified architecture ensures that comparisons remain grounded in concrete geometry.

