4. Universals and Comparative Methodology

The HMVA adopts a statistical universalist stance, following Savage et al. [3] and Mehr et al. [4]. We claim:

· There exists a set of low‑dimensional acoustic features that are probabilistically shared across all known traditions; these form the coordinates of the base space B.
· The realisation of any universal feature is always culture‑specific, described by the internal model space.
· No absolute musical universal is required or assumed.

Testing for statistical universality involves phylogenetic comparative methods that control for common ancestry and geographical proximity. The HMVA integrates these methods at the base‑space level: the distance between base coordinates of two traditions can be corrected for shared ancestry using a Brownian‑motion model along a musical language tree, as in [3]. We note, however, that tree‑based methods are widely debated in cultural evolution due to extensive horizontal transmission (borrowing, hybridisation) [13]; a full treatment would require reticulate network models, which we list as an important future extension. At present, phylogenetic corrections are to be interpreted as provisional.

