Appendix A. Formal Structure of the Stratified Model Space

A.1. The Base Space as a Riemannian Manifold

We construct the base space B as a product of probability simplices with a Riemannian metric that respects the empirical feature correlations reported by Savage et al. (2015). Let there be m binary or categorical features. For feature j with k_j categories, its empirical distribution over a reference set of traditions defines a probability vector \mathbf{p}_j \in \Delta_{k_j}. The overall feature vector is the concatenation \mathbf{p} = (\mathbf{p}_1,\dots,\mathbf{p}_m). To equip B = \prod_{j=1}^m \Delta_{k_j} with a metric that reflects the actual dependency structure, we model the joint distribution of all features as a log‑linear model with pairwise interactions:

\log \mathbb{P}(\text{features}) = \sum_{j} \theta_j^{\top} \mathbf{1}_{\text{feature }j} + \sum_{j<\ell} \mathbf{1}_{\text{feature }j}^{\top} \Theta_{j\ell} \mathbf{1}_{\text{feature }\ell} - \psi(\boldsymbol{\theta},\boldsymbol{\Theta}),

where \mathbf{1}_{\text{feature }j} is the one‑hot encoding of feature j and \psi is the log‑partition function. The Fisher information matrix of this model, taken with respect to the canonical parameters \boldsymbol{\theta},\boldsymbol{\Theta}, defines a Riemannian metric on the manifold of all fully coupled categorical distributions. Projecting onto the marginal distributions of individual features (which are not sufficient statistics in the presence of interactions) yields an intrinsic metric on the product B that is not a simple product of Fisher metrics. Concretely, the metric tensor G(\mathbf{p}) at a point \mathbf{p} can be obtained as the Fisher matrix of the conditional distribution of the full feature vector given the marginal constraints, evaluated at the maximum entropy coupling consistent with the observed correlations. This metric reduces to the product of individual Fisher metrics on the simplices only when the features are independent; in the general case, it includes off‑diagonal blocks reflecting the mutual information between features. (For a detailed information‑geometric treatment of hierarchical models, see Amari & Nagaoka, 2000, Ch. 6.)

Lemma A.1. The metric G is smooth and positive definite on the interior of B, and its geodesics locally characterise the optimal transport of feature probabilities under the joint entropy constraints.

Proof. The model belongs to the exponential family, hence its Fisher metric is smooth and positive definite on the open simplex (Amari & Nagaoka, 2000, Thm. 2.3). The projection onto marginal constraints is a smooth submersion, so the induced metric on the base space is smooth and positive definite where the marginals are non‑degenerate. ∎

This construction ensures that the base space is a genuine Riemannian manifold that respects the empirical interdependence of musical features, preventing the false independence assumed by a naive product metric.

A.2. Stratum‑Specific Model Spaces and Comparison

Definition A.2 (Model Space). For a stratum type T \in \{\mathrm{D}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{R}\}, the model space M_T is a fixed geometric object:

· M_{\mathrm{D}} is the orbifold of all Type‑D pitch‑time structures with a given maximum number of pitch classes and metric periods; formally, M_{\mathrm{D}} = (\mathbb{R}^2 / G_{\text{uni}}) where G_{\text{uni}} is the universal cover of all allowed discrete symmetry groups up to conjugacy.
· M_{\mathrm{C}} is the infinite‑dimensional manifold of spectral envelopes modulo time‑warping (to be constructed in future work).
· M_{\mathrm{R}} is the space of finite automata generating rhythmic timelines.

Each tradition \mathcal{T}_i in a Type‑T stratum is equipped with a structure map \iota_i: \mathcal{T}_i \to M_T that embeds its internal sequences into the model space while preserving the tradition‑specific symmetries. The map is defined by the tradition’s grammar and symmetry group: for a Type‑D tradition, \iota_i sends a sequence of pitch‑class and time symbols to a piecewise‑linear path in \mathbb{R}^2 modulo the tradition’s own G_i, and then canonically embeds P/G_i into M_{\mathrm{D}} via the classifying map of the orbifold quotient.

Proposition A.3 (Comparability within a stratum). Any two traditions \mathcal{T}_i, \mathcal{T}_j in the same stratum can be assigned a structural distance in terms of the geometry of M_T. In particular, for Type D traditions, the Gromov–Hausdorff distance between the images \iota_i(\mathcal{T}_i) and \iota_j(\mathcal{T}_j) provides a well‑defined metric that respects the internal symmetries.

Proof. The structure maps embed each tradition into a common metric space M_T (for Type D, M_{\mathrm{D}} is a complete length space). The Gromov–Hausdorff distance d_{GH}(X,Y) is a metric on isometry classes of compact metric spaces (Burago, Burago & Ivanov, 2001). Although \iota_i(\mathcal{T}_i) may be non‑compact, we may restrict to a compact fundamental domain (one meter cycle) by periodicity, yielding compact orbifolds. The distance is then computed by taking the infimum over all correspondences between the two sets; it is zero iff the orbifold geometries are isometric. If the grammars define different symbol inventories but map to the same orbifold, the distance vanishes, capturing structural identity. ∎

This construction replaces the earlier attempt at a fibre bundle with a concrete, verifiable comparison method that operates only when the traditions are of the same fundamental type.

