Appendix B. Type‑D Orbifold Geometry and the Toy Tradition

B.1. Proof of Proposition 3.1

Proposition 3.1 (Detailed statement). Let a Type‑D tradition be defined by a finite pitch‑class set \mathcal{P}, an octave period (log‑frequency period 1), a transposition group T acting on \mathcal{P}, and a rhythmic period T_{\text{rhythm}}. The pitch‑time symmetries are given by G_{\text{pitch}} = \langle \phi \mapsto \phi + 1 \rangle \rtimes T and G_{\text{rhythm}} = \langle t \mapsto t + T_{\text{rhythm}} \rangle.

1. If the pitch and rhythmic symmetries are independent (i.e., no mode‑dependent meter), the full symmetry group is the direct product G = G_{\text{pitch}} \times G_{\text{rhythm}}. The quotient space is P/G \cong (P/G_{\text{pitch}}) \times (P/G_{\text{rhythm}}), a product of an orbifold and a circle, equipped with the quotient metric. The distance between two sequences is the length of the shortest path in \mathbb{R}^2 modulo the direct product group.
2. If the rhythmic cycle depends on the pitch mode (a coupling), the symmetry group is a semidirect product G_{\text{pitch}} \ltimes G_{\text{rhythm}} with an action of G_{\text{pitch}} on G_{\text{rhythm}} given by a homomorphism \phi: G_{\text{pitch}} \to \operatorname{Aut}(G_{\text{rhythm}}). The quotient is P / (G_{\text{pitch}} \ltimes G_{\text{rhythm}}) and is no longer a product. Geodesic distance is computed in this joint quotient.

Proof. (1) If the symmetries act independently on the factors of P = \mathbb{R}_\phi \times \mathbb{R}_t, the action is (g_{\text{p}}, g_{\text{r}})\cdot (\phi, t) = (g_{\text{p}}\phi, g_{\text{r}} t). The orbit space of a product action is the product of the orbit spaces, and the quotient metric from the Euclidean metric on the product is the product metric (provided the action is by isometries, which it is). Hence P/G \cong (\mathbb{R}/G_{\text{pitch}}) \times (\mathbb{R}/G_{\text{rhythm}}), with the product of the respective quotient metrics. Voice‑leading between two sequences A and B is the infimum of lengths of paths in \mathbb{R}^2 whose projections represent the sequences, constrained to pass through allowed pitch classes. This is a piecewise‑linear geodesic problem in the quotient.

(2) When a coupling exists, the rhythmic period may shift when transposed by a pitch interval, e.g., a new tala for a new raga. We model this as a homomorphism \phi: G_{\text{pitch}} \to \operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb{Z}) (the integer lattice of periods), giving a group action (g_{\text{p}}, k)\cdot (\phi, t) = (g_{\text{p}}\phi, \phi(g_{\text{p}})(t) + k T_0) for some base period T_0. The group law is (g_1, k_1)(g_2, k_2) = (g_1 g_2, k_1 + \phi(g_1)(k_2)). The quotient metric is induced from the Euclidean metric on \mathbb{R}^2 via the quotient by this semidirect product. The resulting space is an orbifold whose singularities reflect the fixed points of the coupled action. ∎

B.2. The Toy Tradition: Full Specification

We detail the synthetic Type‑D tradition used in Section 3.1.

Pitch classes: \mathcal{P} = \{C, E, G\}. Octave equivalence identifies \phi \sim \phi + 1. The transposition group is T = \langle \tau \rangle where \tau(\phi) = \phi + 4/12 (a major third), which cyclically permutes the three pitch classes. Thus G_{\text{pitch}} = \langle \phi \mapsto \phi+1, \phi \mapsto \phi+1/3 \rangle \cong \mathbb{Z} \rtimes \mathbb{Z}_3. The quotient P/G_{\text{pitch}} is a 1‑dimensional orbifold homeomorphic to the circle with three equally spaced cone points of order 3 (Tymoczko 2006, Fig. 2). The orbifold metric inherits a locally Euclidean geometry except at cone points.

Rhythm: meter T=2 beats, with the action t \mapsto t + 2. The joint quotient under independence is the product of the pitch orbifold and a circle of length 2.

HSMM generative model: States s \in \{C, E, G, \text{rest}\}. Initial distribution uniform over pitch classes. State durations: for pitch states, duration is deterministic = 1 beat (corresponding to a quarter note at fixed tempo); for rest, duration = 1 beat with probability 0.8, 2 beats with probability 0.2. Transitions: from a pitch state, the next state is a different pitch class (uniform) with probability 0.7, the same class with probability 0.2, and rest with probability 0.1. From rest, next state is a pitch class (uniform) with probability 1.0. The model generates infinite sequences; for distance computations we consider finite segments.

Voice‑leading distance between two chord sequences: Suppose we have two 4‑event sequences: sequence A = (C, E, G, C) and sequence B = (E, G, C, E). Lift each pitch class to a specific octave (e.g., C4=\phi=0, E4=\phi=4/12, G4=\phi=7/12, C5=\phi=1). The lifted path in \mathbb{R}^2 is piecewise constant in pitch, advancing in time by 1 beat per event. The distance in the quotient is the minimum, over all lifts of both sequences consistent with the equivalence relations (octave, transposition, time shift), of the Euclidean line integral of the difference. For these two sequences, which are related by a uniform transposition of +4 semitones, the distance is zero because A and B are in the same orbit under G_{\text{pitch}} (a transposition). If sequences are not transpositionally equivalent, the distance is the minimal sum of pitch displacements under the best alignment of time and pitch labels. This computation is precisely the standard voice‑leading distance generalised to cyclic time.

