Appendix C. Arnold Tongue Asymptotics and Amplitude Death

C.1. Derivation of Tongue Widths

We consider a single Hopf oscillator forced periodically. Setting \beta = -1 without loss of generality (by scaling z), and ignoring noise \eta for the deterministic analysis, the system in real coordinates (r, \theta) with z = r e^{i\theta} and s(t) real is:

\dot{r} = r(\alpha - r^2) + c \cos(\psi) s(t) \cos\theta + c \sin(\psi) s(t) \sin\theta,

\dot{\theta} = \omega + \frac{c}{r}\bigl(\cos\psi \, s(t) \sin\theta - \sin\psi \, s(t) \cos\theta\bigr).

For weak forcing c \ll 1 and a p\!:\!q resonance \omega \approx \frac{q}{p}\Omega, we use the standard method of averaging. Introduce a slow phase \phi = \theta - \frac{q}{p}\Omega t. The forcing s(t) = \cos(\Omega t) can be decomposed into components at frequency \Omega. After rotating and averaging over the fast time scale, one obtains the averaged equations (Pikovsky et al. 2001, Sec. 7.2):

\dot{R} = R(\alpha - R^2) + \frac{c}{2} \left[ \cos\psi \, C_{p,q} \cos\Phi + \sin\psi \, S_{p,q} \sin\Phi \right],

\dot{\Phi} = \Delta + \frac{c}{2R} \left[ -\cos\psi \, S_{p,q} \cos\Phi + \sin\psi \, C_{p,q} \sin\Phi \right],

where R = \langle r \rangle, \Phi = \langle \phi \rangle, \Delta = \omega - \frac{q}{p}\Omega, and the constants C_{p,q}, S_{p,q} arise from the Fourier coefficients of \cos(\Omega t) at the harmonic that mixes to zero frequency. For the principal resonance 1:1 (p=q=1), the coefficients are non‑zero and the tongue width is proportional to c. For p\!:\!q with q>1, the resonant term involves c^q from the iteration of the map (Arnold 1965), yielding the scaling |\Delta| \leq K_{p,q} c^q for small c. This is the classical result.

High‑q regime: When q is large at fixed c, the averaging must account for the infinite sum of harmonics. The width of the p\!:\!q tongue decays algebraically as q^{-3} for smooth forcing (Pikovsky et al. 2001, Eq. (7.2.15)). To sketch the derivation, the resonance condition \dot{\Phi} = 0 gives \Delta = -\frac{c}{2R} A(q,\psi) \sin(\Phi + \delta). Maximising over \Phi yields |\Delta|_{\max} \approx \frac{c}{2R} |A(q,\psi)|. The Fourier amplitude A of the forcing projected onto the relevant harmonic decays as q^{-3} for a function with Lipschitz derivative, as can be shown by integration by parts. Hence the tongue width at fixed c scales as \sim q^{-3}. This additional decay strengthens the conclusion that only low‑order ratios are dynamically stable.

Lemma C.1 (Combined scaling). For small c and large q, the Arnold tongue width for a p\!:\!q resonance satisfies

\Delta_{\max}(c,q) = O\left( \min\left( c^q, \frac{c}{q^3} \right) \right),

where the first bound dominates for very small c and fixed q, and the second for fixed c and q above a threshold q^* \sim (\ln(1/c)/\ln q).

This lemma formalises the statement in Proposition 3.2.

C.2. Amplitude Death in Coupled Networks

Consider the network of N oscillators with diffusive coupling and a common drive s(t). In the absence of forcing, amplitude death (stable extinction of oscillations) can occur when oscillators are identical or non‑identical and coupling is strong (Mirollo & Strogatz 1990). For our model with forcing, the external drive can counteract amplitude death by supplying energy. The conditions for death are thus parameter‑dependent. A rigorous bifurcation analysis is beyond the scope of this appendix, but we note that for strong heterogeneity in intrinsic frequencies \omega_j and moderate coupling, the network may admit a stable equilibrium at the origin (if \alpha_j can become negative due to the coupling terms). This provides a dynamical mechanism for the absence of entrainment in certain polyrhythmic contexts: if the oscillators are pushed into a death regime, they do not lock to the beat.

