Appendix D. Within‑Stratum Information‑Theoretic Comparison

D.1. Gromov–Hausdorff Distance for Orbifold Sequences

Given two Type‑D traditions i and j with structure maps \iota_i, \iota_j into M_{\mathrm{D}}, we form the sets X_i = \iota_i(\mathcal{L}_i) and X_j = \iota_j(\mathcal{L}_j), where \mathcal{L}_i is the set of all grammatical sequences of a fixed finite length L (or one cycle). These are finite metric spaces under the quotient distance d_i and d_j inherited from M_{\mathrm{D}}. The Gromov–Hausdorff distance is defined as

d_{GH}(X_i, X_j) = \inf_{Z, f_i, f_j} d_H^Z(f_i(X_i), f_j(X_j)),

where the infimum is over all metric spaces Z and isometric embeddings f_i: X_i \to Z, f_j: X_j \to Z, and d_H^Z is the Hausdorff distance in Z (Burago et al., 2001). Because X_i and X_j are subsets of the same model space M_{\mathrm{D}}, we may take Z = M_{\mathrm{D}} with the canonical embeddings, simplifying to the Hausdorff distance in M_{\mathrm{D}}:

d_{GH}(X_i, X_j) \leq \max\left\{ \sup_{x \in X_i} \inf_{y \in X_j} d_{M}(x,y), \; \sup_{y \in X_j} \inf_{x \in X_i} d_{M}(x,y) \right\}.

Equality can be achieved by the canonical embedding if the sets are already optimally aligned (which requires a search over possible time shifts and pitch registrations within the orbifold). In practice, we compute an optimal alignment via dynamic time warping on the fundamental domain to obtain an upper bound.

D.2. Why KL Divergence across Strata Is Unavailable

For traditions in different strata, the event spaces are incommensurable: one tradition’s symbols are pitch classes, another’s are continuous spectral trajectories. The push‑forward into a shared latent space was proposed in v0.2.1, but this required an adversarial alignment whose objective already presupposes a notion of similarity. As no universal metric exists independent of the alignment, we regard cross‑stratum distributional distance as undefined within the present formalism. Instead, cross‑stratum comparison is restricted to the base coordinates (the statistical features), which have a well‑defined probabilistic distance via the Fisher metric on B.

