Appendix F. Phylogenetic Correction and Tree Uncertainty

F.1. Brownian Motion Model along a Tree

Savage et al. (2015) applied a phylogenetic generalised least squares (PGLS) framework to control for shared ancestry. Their model assumes that each of the m features evolves as a continuous trait along a phylogenetic tree \mathcal{T} of musical cultures according to a Brownian motion process. Under this model, the trait vector \mathbf{p}^i \in B for tip i has a multivariate normal distribution with a covariance matrix proportional to the tree’s variance‑covariance matrix \mathbf{C}, where C_{ij} is the shared path length from root to the last common ancestor of i and j. The distance between two traditions corrected for phylogeny is then the Mahalanobis distance \sqrt{(\mathbf{p}^i - \mathbf{p}^j)^\top \mathbf{C}^{-1} (\mathbf{p}^i - \mathbf{p}^j)}, which accounts for expected similarity due to common descent.

Caveat. The pure tree model ignores horizontal transfer (borrowing). Gray & Watts (2017) and others have argued that cultural macroevolution often involves substantial reticulation. Therefore, in the HMVA, we treat phylogenetically corrected distances as one of several comparative lenses, not as definitive. Future incorporation of phylogenetic networks (e.g., using a Neighbor‑Net or admixture graph) will yield more robust corrections.

F.2. Incorporating Uncertainty

The base point b_i is an empirical estimate from a finite corpus, subject to sampling error. A Bayesian approach would place a posterior distribution over b_i, and phylogenetic correction would then propagate this uncertainty. Pending empirical data, we simply note that all distances in B should be reported with confidence intervals reflecting both sampling variance and phylogenetic model uncertainty.

